Subject: [PATCH] common: read gzipped Liberty in Toolbox.get_lib_voltage()

Toolbox.get_lib_voltage() is the only Liberty reader in librelane that opens
the file with a plain open(). With a PDK whose LIB points at .lib.gz, that
takes down OpenROAD.IRDropReport -- the last step of the Classic flow -- with

    RuntimeError: Syntax error in liberty file on line 1.  Unexpected token:

after synthesis, routing, RCX and post-PnR STA have all passed.

Swapping in librelane's own gzopen() is NOT enough: libparse.LibertyParser is
a C++ extension that reads through the stream's fileno(), so it sees the raw
compressed bytes and fails identically (an io.StringIO fails outright with
"UnsupportedOperation: fileno"). The gzipped file has to be materialized.

Reported upstream as https://github.com/librelane/librelane/issues/627
Against librelane 3.1.0.dev3. Drop this once the pinned release carries a fix.
---
--- librelane/common/toolbox.py	2026-08-18 15:39:58.909129817 +0200
+++ librelane/common/toolbox.py	2026-08-18 15:39:58.942702567 +0200
@@ -527,7 +527,22 @@
         :param input_lib: The lib file in question
         :returns: The voltage in question
         """
-        parser = libparse.LibertyParser(open(input_lib, encoding="utf8"))
+        # libparse parses through the stream's fileno(), so a Python-level
+        # decompressing wrapper (gzopen) is not enough for gzipped Liberty:
+        # the C++ side reads the raw compressed bytes straight off the fd and
+        # fails with "Syntax error in liberty file on line 1". Materialize a
+        # plain file instead. Sniffed rather than keyed on the extension, so a
+        # gzipped file named .lib is handled too.
+        lib_path = input_lib
+        with open(input_lib, "rb") as f:
+            is_gzipped = f.read(2) == b"\x1f\x8b"
+        if is_gzipped:
+            mkdirp(self.tmp_dir)
+            lib_path = os.path.join(self.tmp_dir, f"{uuid.uuid4().hex}.lib")
+            with gzopen(input_lib) as src, open(lib_path, "w", encoding="utf8") as dst:
+                shutil.copyfileobj(src, dst)
+
+        parser = libparse.LibertyParser(open(lib_path, encoding="utf8"))
         ast = parser.ast
 
         default_operating_conditions_id = None
